    'nuances' => array(
        'CONSISTENCY: one English term or label gets exactly ONE Italian rendering, and that rendering is identical everywhere it appears — nav, mega-menu, cards, headings, body, footer, forms, buttons, breadcrumbs, <title> and meta description, on that page and on every other page of the site. Before you emit a string, ask whether you have already translated that English elsewhere; if so, reuse it verbatim. Live defects this catches: «Assembly» appeared as «Assemblaggio» in the catalog tree and «Montaggio» on the end-of-line page; «Parla con un esperto» sat on the same page as «Parli con un esperto»; «Resource Hub» was «Hub risorse» in the <title> and «Hub delle risorse» in the nav of the same page.',
        'FIDELITY: never add, drop, strengthen or weaken a claim, modifier, number or qualifier. «Addresses» is not «Eliminates»; «shortly» is not «within minutes»; «helps reduce» is not «riduce»; «designed to» is not «garantisce». Keep every modifier the English carries: «Turbocharger flow bench» keeps «Turbocharger» («banco di flussaggio per turbocompressori»), «diesel turbocharger balancing» keeps «diesel», «Contract balancing as-a-service» keeps «Contract» («conto terzi»), «home countries» keeps «home» («paesi in cui abbiamo sede»), «Speak to an expert now» keeps «now». If Italian needs more words to carry the modifier, use them; shorter is not better.',
        'Keep the grammatical subject stable across a coordinated question and supply the copula English elides: «Machine down or need a hand?» is «La macchina è ferma o ha bisogno di aiuto?», never «Macchina ferma o ha bisogno di aiuto?», which starts on the machine and switches to the reader mid-sentence.',
        '«Follow <Brand>» in a social link, button or aria-label is «Segua <Brand>» in Lei. Never «Seguici su <Brand>»: «su» recasts the company as the platform, and «Seguici» breaks Lei into tu. Only a real platform name (LinkedIn, YouTube, X) may follow «su».',
        'Verify verb and noun government against Italian, not English: «discutere» takes «di» before an interrogative clause («discutere di come possiamo aiutarLa»); «impegno» takes «a»; a person noun takes «di» for their field («tecnici di molteplici discipline», never «in»).',
        'With a mixed-gender coordinated subject Italian takes the masculine plural adjective: «prodotti e soluzioni complessi», never «complesse». Never leave a singular participle dangling against a first-person-plural verb («Forti della competenza dei nostri marchi… progettiamo», not «Forte dell’esperienza… progettiamo»).',
        'When one adjective in a coordinated pair modifies a shared plural noun, inflect both, not just the second. English adjectives are invariant, so agreement errors survive translation unnoticed and are worst where most visible: proofread every headline, CTA and nav string for gender and number.',
        'In a headline built on «noun + relative clause», agree the relative verb with the intended head, not the nearest noun: «Analisi dei dati che forniscono informazioni tempestive» — the claim is about the analytics, «dei dati» is not the head. Whenever the head noun is followed by «dei/delle + plural», re-read the clause and check what it describes.',
        'Drop the definite article before an English company or group proper name used as a subject: «Burke Porter Group ha annunciato…», never «il Burke Porter Group ha annunciato…». Apply it to every occurrence in the passage, not just the first. Keep the article only when the Italian word «gruppo/società/divisione» is actually written out («il gruppo Burke Porter»).',
        'Do not add a definite article where the English is indefinite in form labels and placeholders: «Attach files (optional)» is «Alleghi file (facoltativo)», not «Alleghi i file», which implies specific files. Conversely, restore the article English omits inside a clause: «causando l’interruzione della produzione».',
        'Italian technical noun phrases require the preposition and article English omits by stacking bare nouns. Never copy an English compound as two bare Italian nouns: «Hub delle risorse» not «Hub risorse», «integrazione di sistemi» not «integrazione sistemi», «Analisi dei dati di macchina» not «Analisi dati macchina». Insert the connector a company-plus-division name needs: «La divisione Sistemi di prova e misura di Ascential Technologies affronta…», never four nouns colliding.',
        'Insert the euphonic d whenever «e» or «a» is followed by a word starting with the same vowel: «ed eccellente», «ad Ascentialytics», «ed elettrico». Native writers do this reflexively; two misses on a page read as machine output.',
        'Give every pronoun an antecedent that works in Italian: when English «it» points to an abstraction, restate the noun instead of attaching -la/-lo to the nearest one. Use «dove/in cui» only for places, «ruolo in cui» after a job title.',
        'English quality verbs (elevate, enhance, improve, strengthen, advance) assert a BETTER thing, not MORE of it. «Elevating support, Enhancing value» is «Assistenza superiore, valore maggiore», never «Più supporto, più valore», which is a volume claim and, for a service contract, reads as more billed hours. The same applies to «enhanced», «elevated» and «greater» in any headline.',
        'Keep output, capacity, productivity and throughput distinct — a plant manager reads them as different KPIs: «production output» is «i volumi di produzione», «throughput» is «capacità produttiva», «productivity» is «produttività». Never swap one for another to avoid a repetition.',
        'Place adverbial time, cost and effort phrases where they modify the same thing as in English. «Watch how we integrate … in a few minutes» is the length of the video, not the speed of the process: «Guardi in pochi minuti come integriamo…», never «… integriamo in pochi minuti», which claims the system is built in minutes.',
        'Keep the assertive verb of a claim headline. Any English relative clause with deliver / provide / ensure / cut stays a finite verb in Italian; never collapse it into «per» + noun («Analisi dei dati che forniscono informazioni tempestive», not «Analisi dei dati per informazioni tempestive», which demotes performance to intention).',
        'Never drop a scope anchor that sizes a claim: «the world\'s most innovative companies» is «le aziende più innovative al mondo» — without «al mondo» the superlative reads as a local boast. Same for «global», «industry-leading», «across every market». Preserve superlatives at full strength, never the plain positive degree.',
        '«Empowered» is an agency claim about people, not an equipment claim: «quando le persone sono messe nelle condizioni di…», never «quando le persone hanno gli strumenti per…», which reduces a brand-purpose statement to tooling. Same for «enable» and «equip to decide».',
        'Never drop or add a possessive that carries a claim, and never add a qualifier the source lacks: «our many brands» keeps «nostri» («dei nostri numerosi marchi») and gains no «consolidati»; «your production equipment» keeps «i Suoi impianti di produzione».',
        'An English heading, card title or section label written as a question stays a question in Italian: «What\'s new at Ascential?» is «Cosa c’è di nuovo in Ascential?», not the flattened «Novità da Ascential». The question mark is part of the voice. Never add a full stop an English heading does not have.',
